          "solution": [
            "<b>Classification of Contraceptive Methods:</b>",
            "Contraception includes any deliberate device, medication, or procedure used to prevent conception and unwanted pregnancies. Modern contraceptive techniques are classified into four major categories:",
            "<br/><b>1. Mechanical Barrier Methods:</b>",
            "&bull; Physical barriers that prevent viable sperm from physically reaching and fertilizing the ovum.",
            "&bull; <i>Examples:</i> <b>Condoms</b> (worn over penis by males), female condoms, diaphragms, cervical caps, and contraceptive sponges.",
            "&bull; <i>Crucial Health Advantage:</i> Condoms are the <b>only</b> contraceptive method that also provides effective protection against Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) such as HIV/AIDS, gonorrhea, and syphilis.",
            "<br/><b>2. Chemical / Hormonal Methods:</b>",
            "&bull; Utilize synthetic estrogen and progestin hormones to alter female endocrine feedback loops.",
            "&bull; <i>Mechanism:</i> Inhibit pituitary FSH and LH secretion, preventing ovulation, while thickening cervical mucus to block sperm entry.",
            "&bull; <i>Examples:</i> Oral contraceptive pills ('Saheli', combined pills), hormonal skin patches, injections, and emergency morning-after pills.",
            "<br/><b>3. Intrauterine Contraceptive Devices (IUCDs):</b>",
            "&bull; Small T-shaped devices inserted into the uterine cavity by trained healthcare professionals.",
            "&bull; <i>Examples:</i> Non-medicated (Lippes loop), copper-releasing (<b>Copper-T</b>, CuT-380A), and hormone-releasing (Mirena).",
            "&bull; <i>Mechanism:</i> Copper ions suppress sperm motility and fertilizing capacity, while irritating the uterine endometrium to prevent implantation.",
            "<br/><b>4. Surgical Sterilization Methods (Permanent):</b>",
            "&bull; Highly effective, permanent surgical procedures:",
            "&bull; <b>Vasectomy (in males):</b> The vas deferens is cut and ligated, preventing sperm from entering semen.",
            "&bull; <b>Tubectomy (in females):</b> The fallopian tubes are cut and tied, blocking the egg from meeting sperm."
          ],
          "answer": "Contraceptive methods include: (1) Mechanical barrier methods (condoms, diaphragms) that prevent sperm entry and protect against STIs; (2) Chemical hormonal methods (oral pills) that inhibit ovulation; (3) Intrauterine devices (Copper-T) that prevent implantation; and (4) Surgical methods (vasectomy in males, tubectomy in females) that permanently block gamete transport."
