            "<br/><b>1. Number of Daughter Cells Formed:</b>",
            "&bull; <i>Binary Fission:</i> The parent unicellular organism divides into <b>two</b> equal-sized daughter individuals.",
            "&bull; <i>Multiple Fission:</i> The parent organism divides simultaneously into <b>many</b> (dozens or hundreds of) daughter cells.",
            "2. <b>Environmental Conditions:</b>",
            "&bull; <i>Binary Fission:</i> Occurs under <b>favourable environmental conditions</b> (plentiful food, optimal temperature and moisture).",
            "&bull; <i>Multiple Fission:</i> Frequently occurs during <b>unfavourable conditions</b> (drought, extreme cold/heat), during which the organism encloses itself in a thick protective cyst.",
            "3. <b>Nuclear and Cytoplasmic Division:</b>",
            "&bull; <i>Binary Fission:</i> The nucleus divides once (karyokinesis), immediately followed by cytoplasmic cleavage (cytokinesis).",
            "&bull; <i>Multiple Fission:</i> The parent nucleus undergoes repeated meiotic/mitotic divisions within the cyst to form numerous daughter nuclei before cytoplasm aggregates around each nucleus.",
            "4. <b>Examples:</b>",
            "&bull; <i>Binary Fission:</i> <i>Amoeba</i> (irregular plane), <i>Paramecium</i> (transverse), <i>Leishmania</i> (longitudinal).",
            "&bull; <i>Multiple Fission:</i> <i>Plasmodium</i> (malarial parasite) and encysted <i>Amoeba</i>."
